---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ReadyHandoff from '../../components/ReadyHandoff.astro';
import JourneyDiagram from '../../components/JourneyDiagram.astro';
import { ArrowRight, ExternalLink, Laptop, KeyRound, Globe, Rocket } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Base title="Your first setup, step by step" description="A plain-language Get Ready guide: open your coding agent, hand over setup, handle account sign-in and check a real HTTPS app. Includes Cloudflare and Namecheap instructions.">
<div class="guide-heading"><h1>You don’t need an app idea.</h1><p class="lede">Start with a place for your agent to work. It checks what’s missing and handles the technical setup. You sign in to your own accounts and approve changes that need your say.</p><div class="actions"><a href="#first-step" class="button">Start here <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true"/></a><a href="#whole-journey">See the full product journey</a></div></div>
<details class="guide-flow"><summary>See who does what</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width: 650px)" srcset="/diagrams/get-ready-mobile.svg"/><img src="/diagrams/get-ready.svg" width="1176" height="125" alt="You open your agent. The agent checks your environment. You handle required sign-in or approval. The agent deploys and verifies HTTPS."/></picture><figcaption>Who does what. Sign-in is only needed when an account check asks for it. <a href="/diagrams/get-ready.d2">Editable D2 source</a></figcaption></figure></details>
<div class="guide-steps">
<section id="first-step" class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">1</div><div><h2><Laptop size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Open your coding agent.</h2><p>On your computer, open Codex and choose a project folder. An empty folder is fine. If you already have a project open, stay there.</p><details><summary>I don’t have Codex yet</summary><ol><li>Open the <a href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app">official desktop app instructions <ExternalLink size={15} aria-hidden="true"/></a> and use the download for your computer.</li><li>Install the app, open it and sign in to your ChatGPT account.</li><li>Choose Codex mode, start a new chat and open or create a project folder. The app’s labels may differ by version.</li><li>Return here for the setup handoff in step 2.</li></ol><p class="note">The automated reference setup is currently verified on an Apple Silicon Mac. Other systems need separate agent-led verification. You don’t need to buy an API key to copy this handoff.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">2</div><div><h2><Rocket size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Give the agent the setup job.</h2><p>Keep <strong>Set up my first app</strong> selected, copy the handoff, paste it into the Codex chat for that folder and send it. You can say “I don’t have a domain” or “I already use Cloudflare.” No project brief is needed.</p><ReadyHandoff/><p>Want to look first? Choose <strong>Check what is ready</strong> for a read-only check. After finishing a login, choose <strong>Continue after sign-in</strong> to recheck saved progress before another setup attempt.</p><p class="note">The handoff asks the agent to inspect your actual environment. This website cannot install tools or change your accounts by itself.</p><details><summary>What if GitHub CLI or pnpm is missing?</summary><p>Your agent can prepare the reviewed tools inside your selected project folder on an Apple Silicon Mac with Node 22 and Git. It first checks what already works, verifies the downloads and preserves existing files. The same tools can be reused when it moves into your starter checkout.</p><p>You don’t need to edit your shell settings or choose a package manager. Other systems, missing Git or Node, and account sign-in need separate checks. <a class="underline" href="/docs#workspace-tools">Read the supported preparation step</a>.</p></details><details><summary>What should the agent do next?</summary><p>Check missing tools, GitHub access and the correct Cloudflare account and destination. If you need the starter code, the supported clone operation can start from your empty Codex folder and prepare a new checkout without overwriting your files. The agent checks that checkout separately and can install its pinned local tools.</p><p>For your own source, the agent can explicitly create a new private repository in your personal GitHub account. It checks the owner, name and pushed code, then points your checkout at that repository. Existing names are preserved. The agent can prepare local tools for this unchanged private starter too. Once its local tools work, the agent can prepare a deployment configuration for an unused workers.dev address in your existing Cloudflare account. This changes only a local file. The agent can review and commit that file, deploy the unchanged owned starter to the new address and verify its exact source over HTTPS. After verification, use Init to change its title, introduction and theme. The agent reviews the saved specification and checks the changed live page.</p><p>The agent should name the exact human step when one is needed, save progress privately and continue other checks. It must check again after a fix. Copying code or installing tools never grants permission to deploy.</p></details></div></section>
<section id="account-sign-in" class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">3</div><div><h2><KeyRound size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Sign in when the agent asks.</h2><p>Keep passwords, verification codes and secret keys in the official sign-in screen or your password manager. Never paste them into this website, a specification or a chat message.</p><details id="github-sign-in"><summary>GitHub: where your app’s source lives</summary><p>Being signed in to GitHub in a browser does not sign in the tools in your agent’s folder. Your agent checks that connection first and starts the official login only when it is needed.</p><ol><li>Let the agent start GitHub CLI’s browser login. It gives you a one-time device code and the official verification link. Keep that code in the login flow.</li><li>Open the link, check that the address is <strong>github.com</strong>, and sign in to the account you want to own your app.</li><li>Enter the code on GitHub. Review the <strong>GitHub CLI</strong> application and requested access before authorising it. Stop if the account or request is unfamiliar.</li><li>Wait for the agent’s login command to finish, then tell it you are ready. It checks the selected account and the actual repository permissions.</li><li>Choose <strong>Continue after sign-in</strong> in the handoff above. Rechecking does not retry the stopped setup operation; the agent reviews its saved progress before continuing.</li></ol><p>If you need an account, use <a class="underline" href="https://github.com/signup">GitHub’s own signup</a>. You handle its terms and account verification. If sign-in works but the repository refuses writes, the agent checks the account, repository and required access; signing in repeatedly will not add permission.</p><details><summary>For the agent: use the right tools and account</summary><p>Use the verified GitHub CLI selected for this workspace, including a prepared workspace pair when needed. Check <code>gh auth status --active --hostname github.com</code>. Its JSON output can exit successfully even when authentication is invalid, so do not treat that exit code alone as proof.</p><p>When a new browser login is actually needed, use <code>gh auth login --hostname github.com --git-protocol https --web --skip-ssh-key</code> in an interactive terminal. Review existing token-based authentication before switching methods. Do not request extra scopes or change Git’s global credential helper just to pass the doctor. Check that the runtime has a secure credential store before login: GitHub CLI can otherwise fall back to a plaintext file. Do not select insecure storage for this journey.</p><p>Keep tokens, device codes and account-specific command output out of progress reports. A successful login is separate from repository write access and deployment authority.</p></details><p><a class="underline" href="https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_auth_login">Official GitHub CLI login instructions</a> · <a class="underline" href="https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_auth_status">Authentication status reference</a></p></details><details id="cloudflare-sign-in"><summary>Cloudflare: where your app runs</summary><ol><li>Use <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/">Cloudflare’s dashboard</a> to sign in to your own account.</li><li>If you have more than one account, tell the agent which one owns the domain.</li><li>Complete any account verification on Cloudflare, then let the agent recheck access. Dashboard sign-in and the agent’s Wrangler or API connection are separate; the agent should tell you which connection needs attention.</li></ol><p>You decide whether to accept a new subscription, billing commitment or permission. The agent should explain the cost before asking. An alert is not a hard spending cap.</p></details><details><summary>My domain is already on Cloudflare</summary><p>A domain is your web address, such as <code>example.com</code>. Tell the agent its name. It checks the account, current DNS records and the intended app address before changing anything. You normally do not need to change nameservers again.</p><p>Do not delete existing mail or website records to make a setup check pass. The agent must preserve them or explain a necessary change.</p></details><details><summary>My domain is at Namecheap</summary><p>Only do this when the agent has reviewed the current DNS records and you have agreed to move DNS to Cloudflare. Nameservers tell the internet which service holds your domain’s address book.</p><ol><li>Add the domain in Cloudflare using its <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/dns/zone-setups/full-setup/setup/">full setup guide</a>. Ask the agent to review the imported website and email records first.</li><li>Have the agent check <strong>DNSSEC</strong>, the protection that signs DNS records. If it is active, the agent must explain and ask you to approve the migration step: turn off the old DNSSEC configuration at the registrar and verify the old DS record is gone before changing nameservers. Leaving the old signature in place can make the domain unreachable.</li><li>Copy the two nameservers assigned to <em>your</em> domain by Cloudflare. Don’t copy nameservers from an example.</li><li>In Namecheap, open <strong>Domain List → Manage → Nameservers → Custom DNS</strong>. Enter those two nameservers and save using the checkmark.</li><li>Tell the agent you’ve saved them. It waits for Cloudflare to report the zone active, then checks DNS and your app’s HTTPS address. If DNSSEC was disabled for the move, follow the agent’s instructions to enable Cloudflare DNSSEC, add its new DS record at the registrar and verify it.</li></ol><p><a href="https://www.namecheap.com/support/knowledgebase/article.aspx/767/10/how-to-change-dns-for-a-domain/">Namecheap’s current instructions and screenshots</a> · <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/dns/nameservers/update-nameservers/">Cloudflare’s nameserver instructions</a></p><p class="note">If Cloudflare is your registrar, or DNS is already active there, skip this change. Other registrars use different screens; let the agent find their official instructions.</p></details><details><summary>I don’t have a domain</summary><p>Tell your agent. A domain purchase needs your approval; it should never buy one because a form suggests it. For the supported private starter, your agent can prepare and deploy an unused workers.dev address in an existing authorised Cloudflare account. The maintained launch reference uses its existing Worker and route. New account setup and changes to the app’s source code still need completion.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="guide-step"><div class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">4</div><div><h2><Globe size={24} aria-hidden="true"/>Open your first live app.</h2><p>The agent should give you an HTTPS link to your reference app and evidence that it deployed the intended source. Open the link. The agent should also change the greeting and deploy again. Refresh and check the new greeting.</p><details><summary>Optional: see your setup progress</summary><p>Your agent can give you a redacted JSON progress report. On <a href="/ready#progress-heading">Get Ready</a>, use <strong>Open your agent’s report</strong> and choose that file. The page reads it on your device and shows which checks need attention.</p><p>Use the export, not the agent’s private receipt. A dated report cannot check your computer by itself or grant permission to deploy; ask your agent to recheck after changes.</p></details><p>That proves more than a checklist: the agent can build, deploy and change the app you own. Only then move to <a href="/init">Init</a> to describe the app you want.</p><details><summary>Try the next step: a Hello app</summary><ol><li>Open <a href="/init">Init</a> and choose <strong>Hello World reference</strong>.</li><li>Write a title and introduction. Choose a theme.</li><li>For your verified private starter on workers.dev, keep <strong>A temporary Cloudflare address</strong>. For the maintained reference on your verified custom hostname, choose <strong>An existing domain</strong> and enter that hostname.</li><li>Use <strong>Save and preview</strong>, then <strong>Download spec</strong> and <strong>Copy to Codex</strong>.</li><li>Give the JSON file and handoff to the agent. It reviews the configuration, commits it, deploys through the app’s own release path and verifies the live result.</li></ol><p>The form saves only a local draft. Temporary-address intent can configure your already verified private starter; it creates no new deployment destination. A new-domain choice remains exploration. <a href="/docs#hello-init">Supported Hello configuration details</a>.</p></details></div></section>
</div>
<section class="guide-support"><h2>If something stops.</h2><p>Tell the agent what the screen says, leaving out secrets. It should identify one specific action, such as finishing GitHub login, then resume from the saved progress. Provider outages and routine repairs belong to the agent.</p><p class="note">Working today: one reusable readiness engine, private resume, new personal private source repositories and supported maintained-reference installation/configuration/deployment on the verified Mac path. The unchanged private starter also supports initial workers.dev deployment. New account/domain bootstrap, general source updates/cleanup and hosted API/MCP still need completion. <a href="/release">Read the current evidence</a>.</p></section>
<section class="section guide-vision" id="whole-journey"><h2>From setup to software you own.</h2><p>Get Ready is the first part of a longer journey. Each stage should leave you with something you can use and keep.</p><JourneyDiagram kind="composer"/><div class="journey-stages"><article><h3><a href="/ready">Get Ready</a></h3><p>A working environment, before you need an idea. The supported path checks Codex, GitHub and Cloudflare, prepares your private starter and verifies HTTPS.</p><p class="note">First accounts, more agents, operating systems and registrars still need implementation and verification.</p></article><article><h3><a href="/init">Init</a></h3><p>Choose an app and its settings. Take the portable specification to your agent, then inspect the changed live result.</p><p class="note">Hello supports title, introduction and theme; the photo pair also has layout and collection settings. A richer field editor is still to come.</p></article><article><h3>Build</h3><p>Your agent owns the code, checks and release. You handle sign-in, spending and consequential approvals when they are needed.</p></article><article><h3><a href="/demo">Compose</a></h3><p>The photo guestbook provides approved items to the showcase. Feature one, open its original, then hide it at the source to revoke both public views.</p></article><article><h3>Own</h3><p>Keep the GitHub source, configuration and history. Controlled Hello updates and the photo pair’s pinned releases work today.</p><p class="note">General source updates, cleanup and complete recovery remain open.</p></article></div><p><a href="/architecture">See how the parts connect</a> · <a href="/apps">Try the building blocks</a> · <a href="/release">Read the verified outcomes</a></p></section>
<p class="small">Vendor instructions reviewed 14 October 2026. Official guides include the current provider screenshots; this page does not imitate account or sign-in screens.</p>
</Base>
<script>
// Report links point to optional help. Reveal the named instructions on arrival.
function revealAccountHelp(){
 const id=location.hash.slice(1);if(!['github-sign-in','cloudflare-sign-in'].includes(id))return;
 const details=document.getElementById(id);if(!(details instanceof HTMLDetailsElement))return;
 details.open=true;details.scrollIntoView({block:'start',behavior:'instant'});
}
window.addEventListener('hashchange',revealAccountHelp);
window.addEventListener('pageshow',revealAccountHelp);
revealAccountHelp();
</script>
